# Air Harp — project rules

## What this is
A browser-only instrument: MediaPipe Hand Landmarker tracks hands from the webcam,
pinch-and-drag creates glowing strings, swiping a fingertip across a string plucks it
(Karplus–Strong audio + standing-wave animation + spark particles). No backend.

## Stack (do not change without asking)
- Vanilla JS ES modules + Vite. No React, no TypeScript, no UI frameworks.
- `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` pinned to 0.10.14. WASM path in `src/config.js` must match that version.
- Canvas 2D for rendering. Web Audio API for sound. No audio libraries (no Tone.js) unless asked.

## Architecture
- `config.js` — every tunable number. Never hard-code magic numbers elsewhere; add them here.
- `tracking.js` — camera + HandLandmarker setup + landmark → pixel conversion (handles mirroring).
- `gestures.js` — per-hand state machine (`HandState`) emitting events: pinchStart / pinchMove / pinchEnd / move.
- `strings.js` — `HarpString` model + `StringField` (add/undo/clear/hit-testing). Pure logic, no drawing.
- `audio.js` — `Synth` (Karplus–Strong, cached buffers) + `lengthToFreq`.
- `particles.js` — spark particle system.
- `render.js` — all drawing. Pure functions of state; never mutate game state here.
- `main.js` — wiring + the rAF loop only.

## Conventions
- All coordinates in the loop are canvas pixels (video resolution), already mirrored. Only `toPixels` touches normalized coords.
- Hands are keyed by handedness label ("Left"/"Right"), not array index.
- Hit-testing uses segment intersection between the fingertip's previous and current position — never point-near-line only (fast swipes would be missed).
- Every new gesture needs hysteresis or a cooldown. Assume landmarks jitter.
- Keep the frame loop allocation-light: reuse arrays where easy; no per-frame `new AudioContext`, no per-pluck buffer regeneration (use the cache).
- Performance budget: 60 fps on an M2 MacBook in Chrome with 2 hands and 24 strings.
- Small, focused functions; JSDoc on exported functions. No dead code, no commented-out code.

## When making changes
- Explain which module(s) you're touching and why before editing.
- Don't rewrite working modules wholesale to add a feature — extend them.
- After changes, list how to manually test the feature (gesture + expected result).
